    &lt;h3 id=&quot;description&quot;&gt;Description&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Several methods in &lt;code&gt;sized-chunks&lt;/code&gt; drop elements before updating the length/boundary metadata. If an element&apos;s &lt;code&gt;Drop&lt;/code&gt; panics during the drop, the metadata update is skipped, so the container still treats the already-dropped elements as live. When the container&apos;s own &lt;code&gt;Drop&lt;/code&gt; runs, those elements are visited again — a use-after-free / double-free reachable from safe Rust.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;RingBuffer&lt;/code&gt; methods require the &lt;code&gt;ringbuffer&lt;/code&gt; feature. This is distinct from RUSTSEC-2020-0041 (&lt;code&gt;Chunk::clone&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;insert_from&lt;/code&gt;, fixed in 0.6.3); the methods here are still affected in 0.7.0. The repository is archived with issues/PRs disabled and no fix available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Impact&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CWE-415 (Double Free):&lt;/strong&gt; the same allocation is freed twice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CWE-416 (Use-After-Free):&lt;/strong&gt; a freed allocation is accessed during a repeated &lt;code&gt;Drop&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reachable entirely from safe Rust via &lt;code&gt;catch_unwind&lt;/code&gt; with element types whose &lt;code&gt;Drop&lt;/code&gt; can panic.&lt;/p&gt;


